// Spec §6.5(2): "A class type that has a user-declared destructor or a
// user-declared copy assignment operator, and no user-declared copy
// constructor, has no copy constructor." Spec §11.5(1) forces every
// class to declare a destructor, so no class has an implicitly-defined
// copy constructor -- the reference member changes nothing here, and the
// identical program with an `int` member is rejected identically.
//
// Contrast class_move_construction_allowed_for_class_with_reference_member.scpp:
// §6.4(2) has no such suppression, so *move* construction is allowed.
// The reference-member carve-out belongs to §6.4(3)/§6.5(3), the two
// *assignment* clauses, and to neither construction clause. This case
// previously asserted that copy construction was allowed here, citing a
// `ch04 §4.2` that does not exist.
class RefHolder {
    public:
        virtual ~RefHolder() { return; }
public:
    RefHolder(int& r) : r{r} {
        return;
    }
private:
    int& r;
};

int main() {
    int a = 1;
    RefHolder x{a};
    RefHolder y{x};
    return 0;
}
